\section{Signal Model And Notation} \label{sec: sig}

In the time-domain, the noisy speech signal, $y[n]$, is given by
\begin{equation} \label{equ:b}
y[n] = s[n] + d[n],
\end{equation}
where $s[n]$ and $d[n]$ denote the clean speech and uncorrelated additive noise, respectively, and $n$ denotes the discrete-time index. The noisy speech $y[n]$ is then analysed frame-wise using the short-time Fourier transform (STFT):
\begin{equation}
Y_{l}[k] = S_{l}[k] + D_{l}[k],
\end{equation}
where $Y_{l}[k]$, $S_{l}[k]$, and $D_{l}[k]$ denote the complex-valued STFT coefficients of the noisy speech, the clean speech and the noise, respectively, for time-frame index $l$ and discrete-frequency index $k$. We apply the standard assumption that $S_{l}[k]$ and $D_{l}[k]$ are statistically independent across time and frequency, and follow conditional zero-mean Gaussian distributions with spectral variances $E\{|S_{l}(k)|^{2}\} = \lambda_{s}[l,k]$, and $E\{|D_{l}(k)|^{2}\} = \lambda_{d}[l,k]$, where $E\{\cdot\}$ represents the statistical expectation operator. For convenience, $l$ and $k$ are omitted from the notation unless otherwise indicated. The polar form is used to express $Y$, $S$, and $D$: $Y = Re^{j\phi}$, $S = Ae^{j\varphi}$, and $D = Ne^{j\theta}$, where $R$, $A$, and $N$ are the noisy speech, clean speech, and noise magnitude spectrums, respectively, and $\phi$, $\varphi$, and $\theta$ are the noisy speech, clean speech, and noise phase spectrums, respectively. The \textit{a priori} SNR and the \textit{a posteriori} SNR are defined as
\begin{equation} \label{equ:c}
\xi = \frac{\lambda_{s}}{\lambda_{d}},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\gamma = \frac{R^{2}}{\lambda_{d}},
\end{equation}
respectively. Here, an estimated quantity of a variable is denoted by a hat symbol in order to differentiate from the true value, e.g., $\widehat{N^2}$ is an estimate of the instantaneous noise spectral power $N^2$.


\section{Overview of MMSE-based noise PSD estimators} \label{sec:review}

The MMSE-based noise PSD estimators reported in \cite{R.Yu2009, R.C.Hendriks2010, gerkmann2012improved, Timo.Gerkmann2012, zhang2019novel} are briefly summarised in this section. They are all based on an MMSE estimate of the noise periodogram, which is defined as the conditional expectation: $E\{ N^2|Y, \lambda_{s}, \lambda_{d} \}$. Using Bayes' rule, this can be written as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned} \label{equ:d}
\widehat{N^{2}} &=E\left\{N^{2} | Y, \lambda_{s}, \lambda_{d}\right\}, \\ &=\frac{\int_{0}^{+\infty} \int_{0}^{2 \pi} n^{2} f\left(Y | n, \theta, \lambda_{s}\right) f\left(n, \theta | \lambda_{d}\right) d \theta d n}{\int_{0}^{+\infty} \int_{0}^{2 \pi} f\left(Y | n, \theta, \lambda_{s}\right) f\left(n, \theta | \lambda_{d}\right) d \theta d n}. 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation} 
Under the assumption that the STFT coefficients of clean speech and noise follow complex-Gaussian distributions, $f\left(Y | n, \theta, \lambda_{s}\right)$ and $f\left(n,\theta |\lambda_{d}\right)$ can be written as 
\begin{equation} \label{equ:e}
f\left(Y | n, \theta, \lambda_{s}\right)=\frac{1}{\pi \lambda_{s}} \exp \left\{-\frac{\left|Y-n e^{j \theta}\right|^{2}}{\lambda_{s}}\right\},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation} \label{equ:f}
f\left(n, \theta | \lambda_{d}\right)=\frac{n}{\pi \lambda_{d}} \exp \left\{-\frac{n^{2}}{\lambda_{d}}\right\}.
\end{equation}
Inserting Equations \ref{equ:e} and \ref{equ:f} into Equation \ref{equ:d}, and using the integral relationship of the zeroth order modified Bessel function of the first kind, $I_{0}(\cdot)$ \cite[Eq. 8.406.3, 8.411.1]{I.S.Gradshteyn2007}, we obtain
\begin{equation} \label{equ:g}
\begin{aligned} \widehat{N^{2}} &=E\left\{N^{2} | Y, \lambda_{s}, \lambda_{d}\right\}, \\ &=\frac{\int_{0}^{+\infty} n^{3} \exp \left(-n^{2} / \lambda\right) I_{0}\left(2 n Y / \lambda_{d}\right) d n}{\int_{0}^{+\infty} n \exp \left(-n^{2} / \lambda\right) I_{0}\left(2 n Y / \lambda_{d}\right) d n}, \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
 where $\lambda$ satisfies the relation $\lambda=\lambda_{s} \lambda_{d}/\left(\lambda_{s}+\lambda_{d}\right)$. Using \cite[Eq. 6.631.1, 8.406.3, 9.212.1]{I.S.Gradshteyn2007}, the solution of Equation \ref{equ:g} is obtained as 

\begin{equation} \label{equ:a}
\widehat{N^{2}} =\left[\frac{1}{(1+\xi)^{2}}+\frac{\xi}{(1+\xi) \gamma}\right] R^{2}.
\end{equation}
A temporal recursive smoothing operation is then applied to $\widehat{N^{2}}$ in order to obtain the final noise PSD estimate:
\begin{equation} \label{equ:smoothing}
\widehat{\lambda_{d}}[l,k]=\alpha_{d}\widehat{\lambda_{d}}[l-1,k]+(1-\alpha_{d})\widehat{N^{2}}[l,k].
\end{equation}
From Equation \ref{equ:a}, it is obvious that the MMSE-based Bayesian noise PSD estimator depends on the \textit{a priori} and \textit{a posteriori} SNR. As shown in \cite{R.Yu2009, R.C.Hendriks2010, gerkmann2012improved, Timo.Gerkmann2012, zhang2019novel}, the noise PSD tracking performance is predominately affected by the accuracy of the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate, $\hat{\xi}$. 

In \cite{R.Yu2009}, the decision-directed (DD) approach \cite{Y.Ephraim1984} was employed to estimate the \textit{a priori} SNR and a heuristic bias correction method was proposed to compensate the bias introduced by the DD estimator. In \cite{R.C.Hendriks2010}, a limited maximum likelihood (ML) estimator \cite{Y.Ephraim1984} was used to estimate the \textit{a priori} SNR. A more sophisticated bias compensation term was also derived to correct the bias introduced by the ML estimate. Temporal cepstrum smoothing (TCS) was used in \cite{gerkmann2012improved} to obtain a more accurate estimate of the \textit{a priori} SNR, where a bias correction factor was also derived. In \cite{Timo.Gerkmann2012}, the limited ML estimator was interpreted as a hard-decision, VAD-like estimator \cite{R.C.Hendriks2010}. This was subsequently refined by using a soft-decision speech presence probability (SPP), which required no bias compensation term. The SPP was calculated using a fixed optimal \textit{a priori} SNR. In \cite{zhang2019novel}, an MMSE speech spectral power estimator that incorporates speech presence uncertainty (SPU) and a bias correction factor was proposed to improve the DD approach, producing an improved tracking capability (ImMMSE).


\section{Proposed DeepMMSE method} \label{sec:a}

When estimating the \textit{a priori} SNR for Equation \ref{equ:a}, the previously described methods assume that the noise changes at a slower rate than the speech. Therefore, they are only able to track moderately non-stationary noise sources, and lack the ability to track highly non-stationary noise sources. Motivated by this, we propose a novel MMSE-based noise PSD estimator that employs the Deep Xi framework for \textit{a priori} SNR estimation. The Deep Xi framework does not exploit any underlying assumptions about the speech or noise, and produces an \textit{a priori} SNR estimate with negligible bias. The proposed noise PSD tracker includes the following four stages:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The \textit{a priori} SNR estimate, $\hat{\xi}$, of the noisy speech magnitude spectrum, $R$, is first found using Deep Xi-ResNet. Deep Xi-ResNet is described in Section \ref{secn}.
  
  \item The \textit{a posteriori} SNR estimate is then found using the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate: $\hat{\gamma}=\hat{\xi}+1$.
  
  \item Using $\hat{\xi}$ and $\hat{\gamma}$, the noise periodogram estimate, $\widehat{N^{2}}$, is found using Equation \ref{equ:a}.
  
  \item The final noise PSD estimate, $\widehat{\lambda_{d}}$, is found by applying a first-order temporal recursive smoothing operation to $\widehat{N^{2}}$, with a smoothing factor of $\alpha_{d}$, as in Equation \ref{equ:smoothing}.
\end{enumerate}
Due to the combination of Deep Xi-ResNet and the MMSE noise periodogram estimator, we refer to the proposed method as DeepMMSE henceforth.


\subsection{Deep Xi-ResNet} \label{secn}

In \cite{xi_2018}, a ResLSTM network was used to estimate the mapped \textit{a priori} SNR for each spectral component of the $l^{th}$ time-frame, $\hat{\bar{\pmb{\xi}}}_l$. The mapped \textit{a priori} SNR is described in Subsection \ref{sec:b}. The input to the ResLSTM network was the corresponding noisy speech magnitude spectrum for the $l^{th}$ frame, $\textbf{R}_l$. Here, we replace the ResLSTM network with a ResNet that incorporates one-dimensional dilated causal convolutional units \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1803-01271}. The advantages that the ResNet offers over the ResLSTM network include a reduction in training time and a reduction in the number of required parameters.  

\begin{figure} [ht]
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics[scale=0.7]{fig/resnet_a_priori_snr_estimator.pdf}
    \caption{Deep Xi-ResNet. It consists of a fully-connected first layer, $\textbf{FC}$, followed by $B$ residual blocks, and then a fully-connected output layer, $\textbf{O}$ that employs sigmoidal units.}
    \label{fig:a} 
  \end{center}
\end{figure}

Deep Xi-ResNet, as shown in Figure \ref{fig:a}, consists of $B$ bottleneck residual blocks, where $b=1,2,\ldots,B$ is the block index. As in \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/KalchbrennerESO16}, each block contains three causal dilated convolutional units, where each convolutional unit is pre-activated by layer normalisation \cite{ba2016layer} followed by the ReLU activation function \cite{Nair:2010:RLU:3104322.3104425}. The first and third convolutional units in a block have a kernel size of $1$, whilst the second convolutional unit has a kernel size of $k$. The second convolutional unit employs a dilation rate of $d$, providing a contextual field over previous time steps. As in \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1809-07454}, the dilation rate $d$ is cycled as the block index $b$ increases: $d=2^{(b-1 \bmod(\log_2(D)+1)}$, where $\bmod$ is the modulo operation, and $D$ is the maximum dilation rate. An example of how the dilation rate is cycled is shown in Figure \ref{fig:b}, with $D=4$, and $B=6$. It can be seen that the dilation rate is reset after block three. This also demonstrates the contextual field gained by the use of causal dilated convolutional units.

\begin{figure} [ht]
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics[scale=1.5]{fig/dilation_example.pdf}
    \caption{Example of the contextual field of Deep Xi-ResNet, with $D=4$, $B=6$, and $k=3$.}
    \label{fig:b} 
  \end{center}
\end{figure}


As in \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/KalchbrennerESO16}, $k$ is set to $3$, and $D$ is set to $16$. The first and the second convolutional units have an output size of $d_f$, whilst the third convolutional unit has an output size of $d_{model}$. $\textbf{FC}$ is a fully-connected layer with an output size of $d_{model}$, where layer normalisation applied to the output of $\textbf{FC}$, followed by the ReLU activation function. The output layer, $\textbf{O}$, is a fully-connected layer with sigmoidal units. The following hyperparameters were chosen for the network, as a compromise between training time and performance: $d_{model}=256$, $d_f=64$, and $B=40$.  The ResNet expends fewer parameters than the ResLSTM network, at approximately $2$ million parameters as opposed to $10$ million parameters. The training time for the ResNet is also significantly less than that of the ResLSTM network, at approximately 40 minutes per epoch as opposed to 10 hours per epoch. Details about the training strategy for Deep Xi-ResNet are given in Subsection \ref{secd}.

\subsection{Mapped \textit{A Priori} SNR Training Target} \label{sec:b}

The training target for the ResNet is the mapped \textit{a priori} SNR, as described in \cite{xi_2018}. The mapped \textit{a priori} SNR is a mapped version of the oracle (or instantaneous) \textit{a priori} SNR. For the oracle case, the clean speech and noise of the noisy speech in Equation \ref{equ:b} are known completely. This means that $\lambda_s[l,k]$ and $\lambda_d[l,k]$ in Equation \ref{equ:c} can be replaced with the squared magnitude of the clean speech and noise spectral components, respectively.

In \cite{xi_2018}, the oracle \textit{a priori} SNR (in dB), $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]=10\log_{10}(\xi_l[k])$, was mapped to the interval $[0, 1]$ in order to improve the rate of convergence of the used stochastic gradient descent algorithm. The cumulative distribution function (CDF) of $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]$ was used as the map. It can be seen from Figure 2 (top) in \cite{xi_2018} that the distribution of $\xi_{\rm dB}$ for a given frequency component follows a normal distribution. It was thus assumed that $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]$ is distributed normally with mean $\mu_k$ and variance $\sigma^2_k$: $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]\sim\mathcal{N}(\mu_k,\sigma^2_k)$. The map is given by 
\begin{equation} \label{equa}
\bar{\xi}_l[k] = \frac{1}{2}\Bigg[1 + \textrm{erf}\Bigg( \frac{\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k] - \mu_k}{\sigma_k\sqrt{2}} \Bigg) \Bigg],
\end{equation}
where $\bar{\xi}_l(k)$ is the mapped \textit{a priori} SNR. Following \cite{xi_2018}, the statistics of $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]$ for each noisy speech spectral component are found over a sample of the training set.\footnote{The sample mean and variance of $\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]$ for each noisy speech spectral component were found over $1\,250$ noisy speech signals created from the clean speech and noise training sets (Section \ref{sec:c}). 250 randomly selected (without replacement) clean speech recordings were mixed with random sections of randomly selected (without replacement) noise recordings. Each of these were mixed at five different SNR levels: -5 to 15 dB, in 5 dB increments.} During inference, $\hat{\xi}_l[k]$ is found from $\hat{\xi}_{\text{dB}}[l,k]$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\xi}_l[k] = 10^{(\hat{\xi}_{\text{dB}}[l,k]/10)},
\end{equation}
where the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate in dB is computed from the mapped \textit{a priori} SNR estimate as follows:
\begin{equation}
\hat{\xi}_{\rm dB}[l,k] = \sigma_k\sqrt{2}\textrm{erf}^{-1}\big(2\hat{\bar{\xi}}_l[k] - 1\big) + \mu_k.
\end{equation}

\section{Experimental Settings} \label{sec: exp}

\subsection{Signal Processing} \label{secb}
A square-root-Hann window function is used for spectral analysis and synthesis \cite{237532, Huang:2001:SLP:560905, 4682553}, with a frame-length of 32 ms ($512$ time-domain samples) and a frame-shift of 16 ms ($256$ time-domain samples). The noise PSD was estimated from the 257-point single-sided noisy speech PSD, which included both the DC frequency component and the Nyquist frequency component. For the proposed DeepMMSE method, the \textit{a posteriori} SNR for Equation \ref{equ:a} is estimated from the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate: $\hat{\gamma}(l,k)=\hat{\xi}(l,k)+1$.

\subsection{Training Set} \label{sec:c}
Here, we describe the clean speech and noise recordings used to train Deep Xi-ResNet. The clean speech recordings from the following speech corpora are included in the training set: the \textit{train-clean-100} set from the Librispeech corpus \cite{7178964} ($28\,539$ utterances), the CSTR VCTK corpus \cite{veaux2017cstr} ($42\,015$ utterances), and the $si^*$ and $sx^*$ training sets from the TIMIT corpus \cite{garofolo1993darpa} ($3\,696$ utterances). This gives a total of $74\,250$ clean speech recordings. $5\%$ of the clean speech recordings are randomly selected and used as the validation set. Thus, $70\,537$ clean speech recordings are used in the training set, and $3\,713$ clean speech recordings are used in the validation set. The noise recordings from the following noise datasets are included in the training set: the QUT-NOISE dataset \cite{Dean2010}, the Nonspeech dataset \cite{Nonspeech}, the Environmental Background Noise dataset \cite{7472068, 7590807}, the noise set from the MUSAN corpus \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/SnyderCP15}, multiple \href{https://freesound.org/}{FreeSound} packs,\footnote{Freesound packs that were used: 147, 199, 247, 379, 622, 643, $1\,133$, $1\,563$, $1\,840$, $2\,432$, $4\,366$, $4\,439$, $15\,046$, $15\,598$, $21\,558$.} and coloured noise recordings (with an $\alpha$ value ranging from -2 to 2 in increments of 0.25). This gives a total of $2\,382$ noise recordings. All clean speech and noise recordings are single-channel, with a sampling frequency of 16 kHz (recordings with a sampling frequency higher than 16 kHz are down-sampled to 16 kHz). A description of how the noisy speech is created from the clean speech and noise recordings is given in Subsection \ref{secd}.

\subsection{Training Strategy} \label{secd}
The following strategy was employed to train the ResNet: 
\begin{itemize}
  \setlength\itemsep{0.5pt}
  \item Cross-entropy as the loss function.
  \item The \textit{Adam} algorithm \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/KingmaB14} with default hyper-parameters is used for gradient descent optimisation.
  \item Gradients are clipped between $[-1,1]$.
    \item The selection order for the clean speech recordings is randomised for each epoch.
  \item A total of 175 epochs is used to train the ResNet, where the number of training examples in an epoch is equal to the number of clean speech files in the training set ($70\,537$). 
  \item A mini-batch size of $10$ noisy speech signals.
  \item The noisy speech signals are created as follows: each clean speech recording selected for the mini-batch is mixed with a random section of a randomly selected noise recording at a randomly selected SNR level (-10 to 20 dB, in 1 dB increments).

\end{itemize}

\subsection{Test Set}

Recordings of eight different noise sources were included in the test set. The first is a computer-generated noise source, specifically modulated white Gaussian noise. It is created by modulating Gaussian noise as follows:
\begin{equation}
f(n)=1 + \sin \left(2 \pi n \frac{f_{mod }}{f_{s}}\right),
\end{equation}
where $f_{mod}$ and $f_{s}$ denote the modulation and sampling frequency, respectively, and $n$ is the time-domain sample index. In this study, we set the modulation frequency to $f_{mod} = 0.5$ Hz. Five of the eight recordings were of real-world non-stationary noise sources, including \textit{passing train}, \textit{passing car}, and \textit{traffic} from mutliple FreeSound packs, \textit{street music} (recording no. $26\,270$) from the Urban Sound dataset \cite{Salamon:UrbanSound:ACMMM:14}, and \textit{voice babble} from the RSG-10 noise dataset \cite{steeneken1988description}. Two of the eight recordings were of real-world coloured noise sources, including \textit{factory} and \textit{F16} from the RSG-10 noise dataset \cite{steeneken1988description}. 30 clean speech recordings were randomly selected\footnote{For the noise recordings used in Table \ref{tab:a}, only 10 clean speech recordings were randomly selected.} (without replacement) from the TSP speech corpus \cite{kabal2002tsp} for each noise recording.\footnote{Only adult speakers were included from the TSP speech corpus.} To create the noisy speech, a random section of the noise recording was mixed with the clean speech at the following SNR levels: -5 to 15 dB, in 5 dB increments. 150 noisy speech files were available in the test set for each noise source. The noisy speech signals were single channel, with a sampling frequency of 16 kHz.

\subsection{Evaluation Measures} \label{sec:d}
\textbf{\textit{A priori} SNR estimation accuracy}: The frame-wise spectral distortion (SD) \cite{150426} is used to evaluate the accuracy of an \textit{a priori} SNR estimator, with lower levels indicating a better accuracy. The SD is defined as the root-mean-square difference between the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate in dB, $\hat{\xi}_{\rm dB}[n,k]$, and the oracle case in dB, $\xi_{\rm dB}[n,k]$, for the $n^{th}$ frame:\footnote{$\xi_{dB}[n,k]$ and $\hat{\xi}_{dB}[n,k]$ values that were less than -40 dB, or greater than 60 dB were clipped to -40 dB and 60 dB, respectively.}
\begin{equation}
D^{2}_{l}=\frac{1}{K/2+1}\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}\big[\xi_{\rm dB}[l,k]-\hat{\xi}_{\rm dB}[l,k] \big]^2,
\end{equation}
where $K$ indicates the number of frequency-bins. Average SD levels were found over all frames for the test condition.

\textbf{Noise PSD estimation accuracy}: The spectral estimation accuracy of the noise PSD trackers is evaluated directly using the symmetric mean log-spectral distortion (LogErr) between the reference noise PSD, $\lambda_{d}(l, k)$, and the estimated noise PSD, $\hat{\lambda}_{d}(l, k)$, as follows

\begin{equation}
\textrm{LogErr}=\frac{1}{L K}\sum_{l=0}^{L-1} \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} \left|10 \log _{10}\left[\frac{\lambda_{d}[l, k]}{\widehat{\lambda}_{d}[l, k]}\right]\right|,
\end{equation}
where $L$ denotes the number of time-frames and $K$ indicates the number of frequency-bins. As in previous works, the reference noise PSD, $\lambda_{d}(l, k)$, is obtained from the noise periodogram using a first-order recursive operation with a constant smoothing factor $\alpha_{s} = 0.8$ \cite{R.C.Hendriks2010, gerkmann2012improved, Timo.Gerkmann2012, zhang2019novel}. This smoothing factor is also used by each of the MMSE-based noise PSD trackers (MMSE, SPP, ImMMSE, and DeepMMSE) to compute the final noise PSD estimate, $\widehat{\lambda_{d}}[l, k]$. 

\input{./tables/sd.tex}

\textbf{Speech enhancement performance}: The noise PSD estimators are incorporated into the following speech enhancement framework:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item First, the noise PSD estimate, $\widehat{\lambda_{d}}$, is computed from the noisy speech magnitude spectrum, $R$, using the noise PSD tracker. A constant smoothing factor of $\alpha_{d} = 0.8$ is used for all noise PSD trackers, except for the proposed DeepMMSE method which used $\alpha_{d} = 0$, as no smoothing is required for its noise PSD estimate.
  
  \item Next, the \textit{a posteriori} SNR estimate, $\hat{\gamma}=Y^2 / \widehat{\lambda_{d}}$, is used to find the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate. As in \cite{R.Martin2001,S.Rangachari2006,R.C.Hendriks2010,Timo.Gerkmann2012,zhang2019novel}, the DD approach is used to estimate the \textit{a priori} SNR for the MS, MCRA-2, MMSE, SPP, and ImMMSE methods.\footnote{A smoothing factor of 0.98 was used for the MS, MCRA-2, MMSE, SPP methods, as in \cite{R.Martin2001,S.Rangachari2006,R.C.Hendriks2010,Timo.Gerkmann2012}. A smoothing factor of 0.94 was used for the ImMMSE method, as in \cite{zhang2019novel}.} The smallest allowable value was set to $\xi_{min}=-15~{\rm dB}$ for the DD approach, as in \cite{Loizou:2013:SET:2484638}. For the proposed DeepMMSE method, the ML estimate of the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate is sufficient: $\hat{\xi}=\max \big( \hat{\gamma}-1,0 \big)$. 
  
  \item $\hat{\gamma}$ and $\hat{\xi}$ are then used by the MMSE clean speech spectrum estimator with generalised Gamma priors from \cite{J.S.Erkelens2007} to enhance the noisy speech magnitude spectrum. The MMSE clean speech spectrum estimator assumes that the speech DFT coefficients follow a generalized-Gamma distribution with parameters $\gamma = 1$ and $\nu = 0.6$.

\end{enumerate}
The objective quality and intelligibility of the resultant enhanced speech is then evaluated. The perceptual evaluation of speech quality (PESQ) is used as a metric to evaluate the objective speech quality \cite{recommendation2001perceptual}. Disturbance processing and cognitive modelling are primarily used to determine the PESQ score. The PESQ score ranges from -0.5 to 4.5, with a higher PESQ score implying better speech quality. The short-time objective intelligibility (STOI) measure \cite{5495701, 5713237} is used to evaluate the objective speech intelligibility. It is based on the correlation coefficient between the temporal envelops of the clean speech and enhanced speech in short-time regions. The STOI score ranges from 0 to 1, with a higher STOI score indicating better speech intelligibility.


\section{Results and Discussions} \label{sec: rd}

\subsection{\textit{A priori} SNR estimation accuracy}  

Before evaluating the tracking performance of the proposed DeepMMSE method, we determine if Deep Xi-ResNet is a more accurate \textit{a priori} SNR estimator than Deep Xi-ResLSTM. DeepXi-ResNet is also compared to previous \textit{a priori} SNR estimators, like the DD approach \cite{1164453}, the two-step noise reduction (TSNR) technique \cite{1325979}, harmonic regeneration noise reduction (HRNR) \cite{1415074}, and selective cepstro-temporal smoothing (SCTS) \cite{4518755}. As seen in Table \ref{tab:a}, Deep Xi-ResNet attains the lowest SD level for each tested condition. Deep Xi-ReNet is able to outperform Deep Xi-ResLSTM in terms of \textit{a priori} SNR estimation accuracy, while consuming fewer parameters and requiring less time to train. This indicates that the accuracy of Deep Xi-ReNet will be beneficial to the performance of the proposed DeepMMSE method.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{fig/tracking_mod.pdf}
\caption{(a) Speech corrupted by modulated Gaussian noise at an SNR level of 0 dB. (b)-(c) Noise PSD tracking performance of the noise PSD trackers, including the proposed DeepMMSE method. The noise PSDs are averaged over all frequency bins.}
\label{fig:c}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{fig/tracking_train.pdf}
\caption{(a) Speech corrupted by passing train noise at an SNR level of 0 dB. (b)-(c) Noise PSD tracking performance of the noise PSD trackers, including the proposed DeepMMSE method. The noise PSDs are averaged over all frequency bins.}
\label{fig:d}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Noise PSD tracking evaluation} 

An important feature of any noise PSD estimator is the ability to track non-stationary noise sources. In this subsection, the noise PSD tracking performance of the proposed DeepMMSE method is evaluated against five state-of-the-art noise PSD trackers, including the MS \cite{R.Martin2001}, MCRA-2 \cite{S.Rangachari2006}, MMSE \cite{R.C.Hendriks2010}, SPP \cite{Timo.Gerkmann2012}, and ImMMSE \cite{zhang2019novel} methods. Three clean speech recordings from the test set are concatenated and corrupted with non-stationary modulated Gaussian noise at an SNR level of 0 dB, as shown Figure \ref{fig:c} (a). Figures \ref{fig:c} (b) and (c) show the averaged noise PSD estimates produced by the MS, MCRA-2, MMSE, SPP, ImMMSE, and DeepMMSE methods. The reference noise PSD is also included in Figures \ref{fig:c} (b) and (c). It is obvious that the MS, MCRA-2, and MMSE methods poorly track the modulated Gaussian noise PSD. The SPP method yields an improvement in tracking performance over the MS, MCRA-2, and MMSE methods, but still produces a noise PSD estimate with large bias. The ImMMSE method outperforms the SPP method, demonstrating the ability to track regions of moderately-changing noise levels. The proposed DeepMMSE method produces a noise PSD estimate with negligible bias, and is able to track the abrupt changes in the noise level, e.g., in the time-spans from 3--4, and 5--6 seconds, as highlighted in Figure \ref{fig:c} (c).

\input{./tables/logerr.tex}

Another example is shown in Figure \ref{fig:d} (a), where the clean speech signal used for Figure \ref{fig:c} (a) is corrupted with passing train noise at an SNR level of 0 dB. It is observed again that the proposed DeepMMSE method exhibits an excellent tracking performance, even for rapidly changing noise levels. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:d} (b), the MS, MCRA-2, and MMSE methods again show very large tracking delays. The SPP and ImMMSE methods are able to track the noise PSD during slow changes of the noise level, but again are not able to handle abrupt increases in the noise level, e.g., in the time-span from 1.5--2 seconds, as highlighted in Figure \ref{fig:d} (c). It can also be seen that the proposed Deep MMSE method produces a noise PSD estimate with the least bias.


\subsection{Noise PSD estimation accuracy}
Here, we evaluate the noise PSD estimation accuracy of the proposed DeepMMSE method. The LogErr results for each of the noise PSD estimators are given in Table \ref{table1}. It can be seen that both the MS and MCRA-2 methods exhibit poor noise PSD estimation accuracy for all tested conditions. The MMSE method demonstrates a reasonable noise PSD estimation accuracy, except for noise sources that include rapidly changing noise levels (modulated Gaussian, passing train, and passing car in Table \ref{table1}). The SPP method is not as severely affected by rapidly changing noise levels, and exhibits an improvement in noise PSD estimation accuracy over the MMSE method. The ImMMSE algorithm demonstrates a high noise PSD estimation accuracy, especially at lower SNR levels. However, the proposed DeepMMSE method achieves the highest noise PSD estimation accuracy for each tested condition.

\input{./tables/pesq.tex}
\input{./tables/stoi.tex}


\begin{figure*}[!b]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig/spectrogram.pdf}
\caption{The spectrograms of (a) the clean speech, (b) the noisy speech (clean speech mixed with traffic noise at an SNR level of 5 dB), and the enhanced speech produced by each of the noise PSD trackers: (c) MS, (d) MCRA-2, (e) MMSE, (f) SPP, (g) ImMMSE, (h) LSTM-IRM, (i) Xu2017, and (j) DeepMMSE (proposed).}
\label{fig:e}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Speech enhancement performance} 
Here, the speech enhancement performance of the proposed DeepMMSE method is evaluated within the framework described in Subsection \ref{sec:d}. The objective quality (PESQ) and intelligibility (STOI) scores of the enhanced speech produced by each of the noise PSD trackers are used for evaluation. Table \ref{table2} presents the PESQ scores attained by each of the noise PSD estimators. It can be seen that the proposed DeepMMSE method achieves the highest objective quality scores for each tested condition. The STOI scores obtained by each of the noise PSD estimators are presented in Table \ref{table3}. It can be seen that the proposed DeepMMSE method produces the most objectively intelligible enhanced speech for all tested condition. 

The proposed DeepMMSE method is able to outperform the other noise PSD trackers at all tested SNR levels, and for both real-world non-stationary (e.g. voice babble) and coloured noise sources (e.g. factory), as well as for computer-generated non-stationary noise (e.g. modulated Gaussian). In addition, the proposed DeepMMSE method is also compared to two recent deep learning approaches to speech enhancement: LSTM-IRM, and Xu2017. LSTM-IRM is an LSTM network that estimates the ideal ratio mask (IRM) \cite{chen2017long}, and Xu2017 is a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) clean speech spectrum estimator that incorporates multi-objective learning and ideal binary mask (IBM) post-processing \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/XuDHDL17}. It can be seen in Tables \ref{table2} and \ref{table3} that the proposed DeepMMSE method is able to produce enhanced speech with higher quality and intelligibility scores than LSTM-IRM and Xu2017 for all tested conditions (except for traffic noise at 15 dB.)

\subsection{Evaluation of enhanced speech spectrograms}

In this section, the enhanced speech spectrograms produced by each of the noise PSD trackers are evaluated. A clean speech recording from the test set (Figure \ref{fig:e} (a)) is mixed with traffic noise at an SNR level of 5 dB, producing the noisy speech (Figure \ref{fig:e} (b)). The noisy speech is then enhanced by each of the five state-of-the-art noise PSD trackers (Figures \ref{fig:e} (c)-(g)), LSTM-IRM (Figure \ref{fig:e} (h)), Xu2017 (Figures \ref{fig:e} (i)), and the proposed DeepMMSE method (Figure \ref{fig:e} (j)). It can be seen that the proposed DeepMMSE method achieves the best noise suppression, while preserving the formant information of the speech (Figure \ref{fig:e} (j)). The MS method demonstrates the worst noise suppression performance (Figure \ref{fig:e} (c)). Although the MCRA-2 method demonstrates improved noise suppression over the MS method, the speech is distorted due to an overestimation of the noise level (Figure \ref{fig:e} (d)). Both the ImMMSE method (Figure \ref{fig:e} (g)) and the SPP method (Figure \ref{fig:e} (f)) demonstrate a similar noise suppression performance, with both outperforming the MMSE method (Figure \ref{fig:e} (e)). It can be seen in Figures \ref{fig:e} (h) and (i) that LSTM-IRM and Xu2017 both under suppress multiple spectral components.

\section{Conclusion} \label{sec: cfc} 

A critical component of any speech enhancement systems is the estimation of the noise PSD. The performance of an MMSE-based noise PSD tracking method heavily depends on the accuracy of the used \textit{a priori} SNR estimator. Motivated by this, we investigate how an MMSE-based noise PSD tracker can benefit from a deep learning approach to \textit{a priori} SNR estimation. The proposed noise PSD tracking method, called DeepMMSE, is evaluated using a variety of real-world non-stationary and coloured noise sources at multiple SNR levels. Our results show that the proposed DeepMMSE method is able to significantly outperform other noise PSD trackers in terms of spectral tracking accuracy. As the proposed method does not exploit any assumptions about the characteristics of the speech or noise, it is able to track sudden changes in the noise level. Furthermore, the proposed DeepMMSE method is able to yield higher speech enhancement objective quality and intelligibility scores than other noise PSD tracking methods, as well as two deep learning approaches to speech enhancement. 

Recently, perceptually guided training has been exploited to increase speech enhancement performance \cite{koizumi2017dnn,zhao2018perceptually}. This may be investigated in future work to obtain a further improvement in performance. In this work, the \textit{a posteriori} SNR estimate is calculated from the \textit{a priori} SNR estimate. Further improvements in performance may be obtained by using a deep learning approach to estimate the \textit{a posteriori} SNR directly. Moreover, the focus of this paper is on modifying only the magnitude of the STFT components. Researchers have found that phase provides additional information which can help distinguish noise outliers from speech \cite{gerkmann2015phase}. Incorporating this into the proposed estimator may improve its performance.  


